            <ArticleTitle>Interleukin-4 (IL-4) pathway.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>cm9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Interleukin-4 (IL-4) is a cytokine produced by T(H)2 type helper T cells and by mast cells, basophils, and eosinophils. This cytokine can elicit many responses, some of which are associated with allergy and asthma. Studies with long-term cell lines and primary cells have revealed differences in the signaling between these two experimental systems. Understanding these differences is important because therapeutic strategies targeting IL-4 and its signaling pathways are currently being tested to treat allergy and asthma.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
